e anything  that's not showing up on my screen but  basically you don't see anything if you  order by collection order  what i can do  is i can figure out um  can i order the cells let me say in a  way that i can reconstruct one cycle of  the oscillation which we refer to as a  base cycle so here i'm showing you two  base cycles because i don't have any  time order i can't figure out does  cell 4 go here  or does it go in a different spot that's  identical  to it like near cell 2 right here i  can't figure out those differences right  but i can get some information on the  cell order  that allows me to reconstruct one cycle  of the oscillation which we call the  base cycle that order is called a cyclic  order  okay so how do we do that well first  this is an approach called osco and the  first thing we do is we screen for  candidate oscillators so independent of  order if you have genes that are  oscillating with similar frequencies and  you plot one against the other you will  see them showing up as some sort of  ellipse so we fit a model that looks for  patterns we call the paired sine model  but it basically it looks at  pairs of genes and it fits an elliptical  function to those pairs and it pulls out  ones where the residuals are small we  call those candidates so those are  oscillatory candidates  given the candidates we cluster them  into clusters and the clusters are based  on uh this paired sign model score so  the residuals again and ones that have  small residuals are oscillating with  similar frequencies they may be phase  shifted in fact they they almost are  almost always are phase shifted  and then from that we can recover the  order within each cluster okay within  each group of genes that has similar  frequency but its phase shifted we  recover the cyclic order which is again  the order of cells that allows us to  reconstruct one cycle of the oscillation  which we call base cycle and we do that  by extending  um  an algorithm called the nearest  insertion algorithm that was developed  for the traveling salesman problem so  with oscope we recognize a connection to  the traveling salesman problem  excuse me which is that given a list of  cities you only know the distances  between each pair of cities and i'm  showing you know pairs of cities below  and their distances and the goal is to  find an optimal route that visits each  city exactly once and returns to the  origin city  and optimal route is going to minimize  the overall distance travel  here  it's similar to the traveling salesman  problem but we have a gene cluster and  we want to find the optimal route  through all the cells  that returns to the first cell because  again we are we're trying to um  reconstruct an order i'm just going to  move that around where we start at cell  one and we move around and we come back  to cell one so in that way it's like  returning to the origin city  the optimal route is going to minimize  what  well that's sort of where the extension  comes in it minimizes expression  differences between observed and  baseline oscillations now the nearest  insertion algorithm was an algorithm  developed that um to address the  trembling salesman problem that works  pretty well we extended it to  accommodate our specific problem here so  we call that the extended nearest  insertion algorithm  just a sec i'm getting a question  okay so the question is what is a  bayesian approach and i think i'm going  to  get through that and then i'll answer  um that question  at the end  um okay so in terms of results i i want  to show you what what did we do what did  we do to validate results well we did  simulation studies of course i won't be  showing you the results of that today  but they're in our paper should you be  interested  um we also took  data where we know the answer from time  course experiments people have done a  bunch of time course experiments in a  bunch of different settings as  i assume everyone listening knows and  they've identified genes that are  oscillating and then in many cases they  validated those genes experimentally so  we took  a data set a microarray data set from  long ago  where it was a pretty large sample size  and they had validated the authors had  validated  a gold standard list of oscillating  genes  we ignore the time course order so we  just shuffle the data and we apply o  scope to see what we can recover  and um four of those genes are shown  here so on the right top panel  we show the known time course order and  and the actual profiles for each of  those four genes  and on the left we show the order  recovered by osco  you can see these genes are oscillating  with similar frequencies we can recover  that we can also recover the phase  shifts pretty well here  and here  where it's the highest point in that  base cycle that we see for each of these  genes  a second validation that we did is we  had identified a gene cluster that  seemed to be involved in cell cycle  because many of the genes were known to  be involved in cell cycle and so we  wanted to see if the order that  we recovered using oscope was consistent  with cell cycle and so the thompson lab  did an experiment where they labeled  cells and they could tell which part of  the cell cycle each cell was in and  we're showing here the eni extended  nearest insertion output and recovered  order on the left for four genes  in that cluster  and um  those same  genes  with labeled cells on the right hand  side so we have cells in the s phase  labeled blue and then g2 and g1 and you  can see that the order recovered by  oscope is in fact consistent with cell  cycle phases  there are a number of other validations  that i won't talk about today  so in summary and then i'll take the  time here at this  at this uh  break before i just close with a few  more slides to answer that question  about a bayesian approach so in summary  um  as i as i said in the first part of the  talk ebc was designed for inference uh  on identifying differentially expressed  isoforms it also works for identifying  differentially expressed genes  um osco  is a statistical pipeline for  characterizing oscillatory genes in a  snapshot single cell rna-seq experiment  and we're going to cross this one out  for a minute because that's what i'm  going to talk about in just a second i'm  sorry it's there um  let me just say briefly a bayesian  method  is one in which you introduce a prior  component so that you can evaluate  posterior probabilities  of  whatever you're interested in in our  case it's differential expression  and  we assume that the the targets of  interest the latent expression levels  are themselves random variables that's i  guess a quick way to think about it okay  in terms of availability uh ebc is in  bioconductor it's in galaxy and there's  a graphical user interface  the details are in bioinformatics in  2013 ebc hmm bioinformatics 2015.  what i'll  briefly close with is magic  that's also coming out in bioinformatics  in 2015 and oh scope is hopefully coming  out soon  so do i not have  okay so that's why that was let me just  say i thought i had a few other slides  on on magic um  magic is an approach that we developed  for identifying driver mutations in  cancer  um  and  it  so a number of methods are available for  identifying driving mutations in cancer  the earliest ones are what we call  frequency-based approaches where if a  gene is frequently mutated it has a  number of mutations a a very high number  of mutations across a patient's sample  it is called a driver  the trick with those methods is that you  have to figure out how big is big and so  you need to estimate a background  mutation rate and that background  mutation rate varies across the genome  so those methods are really focused on  identifying good models developing good  models and estimating uh accurate  background mutation rates so that they  can tell that this gene uh is  has a number of mutations that's far and  above what we would expect given this  background mutation rate that's  frequency-based approaches  then there are another class of  approaches that look at the functional  impact scores of various mutations and  if  a gene is enriched across a patient  population for mutations that have very  high functional impact those genes are  called drivers  so third class about position so where  in the gene have  mutations  compiled are they in important positions  in terms of amino acid  product in terms of the protein that's  produced  and so magic is an approach that unifies  those three considerations for  identifying driver genes in cancer and  it wasn't in my title but i wanted to  talk about it because we're excited  about it and we hope that  it can improve the current state of the  art in that area  so with that i will um  thank you for listening  um i'll thank my collaborators again in  the thompson lab ron stewart um  lee fong 2 should be on here victor  roddy helped with the earlier work my  former graduate student ning lang is the  first author on the ebc  work keegan courthouse first author on  magic and is helping with single cell  work now and working on problems in  single cell area as is rhonda walker um  and i am often hiring postdocs so with  that i'll  thank you for your time and i'm happy to  take more questions  thank you dr kendorsky for that  informative presentation before we get  started on a question and answer session  i'd like to remind our audience how to  submit questions you can submit  questions by typing them in the q a box  which can be found by clicking on the  green q a button at the lower left of  the presentation window  and  answered several questions  there appear to be no additional  questions from the audience  give them just a minute um  well as there appear to be no no further  questions from the audience we'll  conclude the presentation  today's webcast will be available for  on-demand viewing through november 13  2015 you'll receive an email from lab  roots alerting you when this webcast  will be available for replay we invite  you to forward that announcement to your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event see you next time goodbye
 

